This is a fork of fx (https://github.com/vercel-labs/fx), licensed Apache-2.0.

Every Unbound change is listed here. `sdk/tests/test-unbound-fork.mjs` asserts
the load-bearing ones, so a sync with upstream that drops one fails CI instead
of reaching production. If you take upstream's side of a file below, re-apply
the change and run that test before you package a release.

## Read this before syncing with upstream

A sync has already cost us these twice. The 2026-09-18 sync merged with the
`ours` strategy, which discards the other side wholesale: the tree looked fine,
CI was green, and production lost skills and every MCP server with more than 63
tools. After any merge or rebase from upstream, run:

    node --experimental-wasm-jspi sdk/tests/test-unbound-fork.mjs

## Changes, all confined to the browser (wasm) host

- src/core/tooling/host_tool_runtime.zig: take up to 128 host tools, not 64.
  Linear alone lists 74, and at 64 its whole catalog is refused.
- sdk/mcp.js, sdk/mcp-browser.js, sdk/fx-sdk.js: the same ceiling on the JS
  side, 128, 127 and 128. The error a server over the limit produces says how
  many tools fx can take, rather than calling the catalog invalid.
  `maxHostTools` in sdk/fx-sdk.js is the one the page actually hits: at 64,
  setTools refuses a 74-tool server whole, so `/mcp` prints its catalog while
  the model reports no MCP tools at all.
- src/core/hosts/runtime_profile.zig: enable the `skills` capability for the
  wasm host profile. Upstream disables it because a browser has no filesystem.
- sdk/memfs.js (new): a read-only in-memory WASI filesystem.
- sdk/fx-sdk.js: back the WASI read calls (path_open, fd_readdir, fd_read,
  fd_seek, fd_pread, fd_prestat_*, path_filestat_get, fd_filestat_get) with
  that filesystem when the host supplies `files`. Without it the wasm prints
  "Skills are unavailable in this host because filesystem access is not
  provided" and /skills, skill listing and loading by name all stop working.
- src/core/skills/skill_runtime.zig: skip realpath canonicalisation on wasi,
  which has no realpath(3).
- src/main.zig: a wasm skill root policy. A browser host that supplies files
  scans the same workspace roots as native, and has no home directory, so no
  managed or global roots. Without it discovery walks nothing and /skills is
  empty. The interactive paths take the same policy.
- src/core/hosts/js_host_term_tools.zig, src/main.zig,
  src/core/app/app_commands.zig, src/core/app/app_agent_runtime.zig: let the
  browser terminal take host tools the way createFxAgent does, hand `/mcp` to
  the host, and load the host's tools again before a prompt when they change.
- sdk/mcp-install.js, sdk/mcp-oauth.js, sdk/mcp-browser.js, sdk/mcp-host.html,
  sdk/mcp-callback.html: run MCP servers in the page. `/mcp add` takes the
  arguments `claude mcp add` takes. An npm server runs in a sandboxed frame and
  a classic worker, from a tarball the browser fetches. A remote server connects
  over HTTP or SSE and signs in with dynamic client registration and PKCE.
  Every tool call passes the gate the host supplies.
- src/core/hosts/js_host_tool_review.zig (new), src/core/tooling/tool_admission.zig,
  src/core/app/app_agent_runtime.zig, src/main.zig,
  src/core/permissions/permission_request.zig, sdk/fx-sdk.js, sdk/fx-sdk.d.ts:
  `reviewToolCall`, a pre-tool hook for the browser terminal. The page answers
  allow, ask or deny for every shell call and host tool before fx's own
  permission step. Ask uses fx's own confirm prompt with the page's reason;
  deny and a declined ask fail the call with the reason and context. A hook
  that throws fails open. Unbound's policy sandbox enforces org policy with it.
- src/core/agent/worker_runtime.zig, src/main.zig: a cooperative permission
  wait. The browser terminal runs the agent on its only thread, so a permission
  prompt the agent opened blocked forever on a condition variable nothing could
  signal. The waiting worker now turns the terminal's loop itself until the
  prompt is answered. The `ask` above depends on it.
- src/ui/footer/approval_ui.zig, src/ui/footer/surface_frame.zig,
  src/ui/approval_screen.zig, src/core/permissions/permission_request.zig,
  src/core/tooling/tool_admission.zig: a multi-line approval explanation. An
  `ask` reason is often a 20-line box; upstream gives the explanation one row
  and 256 bytes, so the prompt showed `\x0a┏━━━…` and nothing else. The
  explanation now keeps its line breaks (every other control still escaped),
  takes up to 40 lines and 8 KiB, and the prompt grows a row per line, down to
  a closing `…` row when the terminal is too short for the choices.
- src/core/permissions/command_admission.zig, src/core/agent/runtime/orchestrator.zig,
  src/core/tooling/tool_admission.zig, src/core/hosts/js_host_tool_review.zig,
  sdk/fx-sdk.js, sdk/fx-sdk.d.ts: `summary` on an ask or deny review, and a
  one-line transcript detail for a call the review stopped. Upstream derives
  that line from the tool result, which printed the reason's `\x0a`s. The
  model still gets the full reason and context.
- sdk/README.md: documents `reviewToolCall`.
- sdk/mcp.js, sdk/mcp-browser.js, sdk/fx-sdk.d.ts: every MCP tool carries
  `source`, the server and tool name it really calls. The model-facing name is
  normalised, truncated and de-duplicated, so a host that must decide about a
  call cannot read the real identity back out of it, and a policy written
  against the server's own tool name would never match.
- src/core/shared/debug_trace.zig: turn and step IDs count up in the wasm
  build. Upstream returns a fixed 1 there, which gave every turn the same tool
  presentation group, so each later turn's tool calls folded into turn 1's
  summary and no tool line showed after the first message.

The model reaches a skill through the host tool the page supplies
(`createBrowserSkillTools`), not through a builtin: the browser tool set
advertises `shell` alone, as upstream has it. An earlier revision of this file
claimed a builtin `skill` tool and a plain-path default for the wasm URLs in
sdk/browser.js. Neither is in the tree, both went in the 2026-09-18 sync, and
nothing missed them, because the page passes its own wasm and host URLs and
its own skill tool. They are recorded here as dropped, not as wanted.

Purpose: make fx skills load and run in a browser host exactly as they do in
native fx, and let a browser terminal use host tools (for MCP) as the browser
agent already can, with servers added through fx's own `/mcp` command.
